Resolve thread-local variables found through the ELF symbol table.

`expr <tls_var>` works when the variable has debug info: DWARF gives it a
DW_OP_form_tls_address location, which DWARFExpression resolves through
Thread::GetThreadLocalData(). With no debug info the variable is only found
in the symbol table, and there ObjectFileELF lowers STT_TLS to
eSymbolTypeData and drops the TLS-ness -- lldb has neither an eSymbolTypeTLS
nor a TLS flag on Symbol. st_value of an STT_TLS symbol is an offset inside
the module's TLS block, so treating it as an address hands the expression a
bogus pointer:

    (lldb) p &tls_gd
    (void **) 0x000000000000000c        <- st_value, not an address
    (lldb) p tcache
    error: Couldn't apply expression side effects : Couldn't dematerialize a
           result variable: couldn't read its memory

Add Thread::GetThreadLocalSymbolAddress(), which recovers the ELF symbol type
from Symbol::GetFlags() -- ObjectFileELF packs `st_other << 8 | st_info`
there, so the low four bits are the symbol type -- and feeds the file address
to GetThreadLocalData(), the same value the DWARF path pushes. Sitting on
Thread keeps it next to GetThreadLocalData() and avoids duplicating the ELF
detail across callers.

Use it where such a symbol is turned into an address:

  - Materializer's EntitySymbol::Materialize(), which is what actually backs
    `p <tls_var>`: it recomputes the address from the symbol, so fixing only
    the decl map has no effect on the result.
  - ClangExpressionDeclMap::GetSymbolAddress().

AddOneGenericVariable() is deliberately left alone: it stores the address in
ParserVars::m_lldb_value, whose only reader is the function-decl path in
GetFunctionInfo(), so resolving TLS there computes a value nothing consumes
and costs an extra DTV walk per expression.

Both call sites keep their previous behaviour when the lookup fails, so a
symbol whose TLS block cannot be located is no worse off than before.

Not architecture-specific: this reproduces identically on x86-64 and aarch64,
and is unrelated to LLDB_REGNUM_GENERIC_TP -- the thread pointer itself reads
back fine.

